      "body_html": "Here are some photos of various clones of S. montana taken 7/21/13.  As you scroll down the photos, take a look at the subtle differences between different plants:<br/><br/>S. montana Transylvania Co, NC (a clone from Wes Buckner).  I'm really enjoying the shape of these traps...the dark red spots at the \"bottom\" the \"hood\" also sticks out:<br/><a href=\"http://s1350.photobucket.com/user/pitcherperfect1/media/July%2013/IMG_0208_zps2d11b58c.jpg.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"http://i1350.photobucket.com/albums/p767/pitcherperfect1/July%2013/IMG_0208_zps2d11b58c.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/></a><br/><br/><br/>Another shot, overview pic Wes Buckner's clone:<br/><a href=\"http://s1350.photobucket.com/user/pitcherperfect1/media/July%2013/IMG_0210_zps3f586386.jpg.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"http://i1350.photobucket.com/albums/p767/pitcherperfect1/July%2013/IMG_0210_zps3f586386.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/></a><br/><br/><br/><br/>this is clone AA, the one that can produce brilliant colors during the fall.  In fact, all the montanas seem to be able to turn a dark red under optimal conditions.  Clone AA  is fast growing and forms a very large clump in little time:<br/><a href=\"http://s1350.photobucket.com/user/pitcherperfect1/media/July%2013/IMG_0211_zpsd9e98cd8.jpg.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"http://i1350.photobucket.com/albums/p767/pitcherperfect1/July%2013/IMG_0211_zpsd9e98cd8.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/></a><br/><br/><br/>close-up of clone AA:<br/><a href=\"http://s1350.photobucket.com/user/pitcherperfect1/media/July%2013/IMG_0212_zps44987fea.jpg.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"http://i1350.photobucket.com/albums/p767/pitcherperfect1/July%2013/IMG_0212_zps44987fea.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/></a><br/><br/>Clone BB, a relative of Clone AA:<br/><a href=\"http://s1350.photobucket.com/user/pitcherperfect1/media/July%2013/IMG_0217_zps09e54803.jpg.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"http://i1350.photobucket.com/albums/p767/pitcherperfect1/July%2013/IMG_0217_zps09e54803.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/></a><br/><br/>clone BB:<br/><a href=\"http://s1350.photobucket.com/user/pitcherperfect1/media/July%2013/IMG_0218_zpse0db3880.jpg.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"http://i1350.photobucket.com/albums/p767/pitcherperfect1/July%2013/IMG_0218_zpse0db3880.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/></a><br/><br/>clone BB:<br/><a href=\"http://s1350.photobucket.com/user/pitcherperfect1/media/July%2013/IMG_0216_zpsda939937.jpg.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"http://i1350.photobucket.com/albums/p767/pitcherperfect1/July%2013/IMG_0216_zpsda939937.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/></a><br/><br/><br/>Can't remember which clone this is, but check out how there's a pitcher on this one that looks like a regular venosa:<br/><a href=\"http://s1350.photobucket.com/user/pitcherperfect1/media/July%2013/IMG_0214_zps97b748f3.jpg.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"http://i1350.photobucket.com/albums/p767/pitcherperfect1/July%2013/IMG_0214_zps97b748f3.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/></a><br/><br/><br/>And here's the same plant, zoomed out-notice how some of the other pitchers look more montana than \"venosa-like\"<br/><a href=\"http://s1350.photobucket.com/user/pitcherperfect1/media/July%2013/IMG_0215_zpse366a009.jpg.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"http://i1350.photobucket.com/albums/p767/pitcherperfect1/July%2013/IMG_0215_zpse366a009.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/></a><br/><br/>nice little clump-can't remember which clone this is:<br/><a href=\"http://s1350.photobucket.com/user/pitcherperfect1/media/July%2013/IMG_0213_zps66412ee2.jpg.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"http://i1350.photobucket.com/albums/p767/pitcherperfect1/July%2013/IMG_0213_zps66412ee2.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/></a><br/><br/>Last but not least, the population of montanas.  I wanted to take photos of each one, but they're growing so close to each other that I was breaking pitchers every time I took one out:<br/><a href=\"http://s1350.photobucket.com/user/pitcherperfect1/media/July%2013/IMG_0219_zpsf3700e7b.jpg.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"http://i1350.photobucket.com/albums/p767/pitcherperfect1/July%2013/IMG_0219_zpsf3700e7b.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%;\"/></a>",
      "body_text": "Here are some photos of various clones of S. montana taken 7/21/13.  As you scroll down the photos, take a look at the subtle differences between different plants:\nS. montana Transylvania Co, NC (a clone from Wes Buckner).  I'm really enjoying the shape of these traps...the dark red spots at the \"bottom\" the \"hood\" also sticks out:\nAnother shot, overview pic Wes Buckner's clone:\nthis is clone AA, the one that can produce brilliant colors during the fall.  In fact, all the montanas seem to be able to turn a dark red under optimal conditions.  Clone AA  is fast growing and forms a very large clump in little time:\nclose-up of clone AA:\nClone BB, a relative of Clone AA:\nclone BB:\nclone BB:\nCan't remember which clone this is, but check out how there's a pitcher on this one that looks like a regular venosa:\nAnd here's the same plant, zoomed out-notice how some of the other pitchers look more montana than \"venosa-like\"\nnice little clump-can't remember which clone this is:\nLast but not least, the population of montanas.  I wanted to take photos of each one, but they're growing so close to each other that I was breaking pitchers every time I took one out:",
